Description: Context menus the Windows way: open on release, click through to close
 GTK applications pop up a context menu from the press of the secondary
 button, and the menu then follows the X11 press-drag-release model: drag
 onto an item and let go to choose it. Windows takes two steps. The menu
 appears when the button is released and stays open until a second,
 separate click. Someone used to Windows lets go as soon as the menu
 appears and picks whatever item is under the pointer; GTK's 500ms guard
 does not help, because it is cleared as soon as the pointer moves into an
 item.
 .
 gtk_main_do_event() now follows the secondary button in event order, and
 a top-level menu popped up while it is down is held back and shown once
 the release has been dispatched. No release is left to choose with. A
 press of another button, a popdown or a drag starting from the press
 cancels the held-back menu.
 .
 On Windows a press outside an open context menu also closes the menu and
 still reaches what is under the pointer, so a right click elsewhere opens
 a menu there in one click and a left click elsewhere acts there. GTK
 spends that press on closing the menu. During the menu's grab GDK reports
 such a press on the menu's own window, so the window under the pointer is
 asked of the device; the menu is cancelled, as Escape would, and the press
 is dispatched to that window. Menus dropped down from a button, menu bars
 and keyboard-opened menus keep their behaviour, so a click on the button
 that opened a menu only closes it.
Author: AltarsCN <altarscn2@gmail.com>
Forwarded: not-needed
Last-Update: 2026-10-03
